WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.785
Authentication answers the question, who
is making this request.

2
00:00:06.865 --> 00:00:14.273
Minimal APIs use the same ASP.NET Core
authentication system as the rest of the

3
00:00:14.353 --> 00:00:15.363
platform.

4
00:00:15.443 --> 00:00:18.361
Depending on the application,

5
00:00:18.441 --> 00:00:22.931
that may involve bearer tokens for API
clients,

6
00:00:23.011 --> 00:00:29.633
cookies for browser-based workflows,
external identity providers,

7
00:00:29.713 --> 00:00:32.519
or other configured schemes.

8
00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:38.435
The important point is that authentication
must be explicit.

9
00:00:38.515 --> 00:00:46.372
A Minimal API endpoint should not rely on
local development identity assumptions,

10
00:00:46.452 --> 00:00:54.084
trusted test headers, route naming, or a
client convention as proof of identity.

11
00:00:54.164 --> 00:01:01.908
The identity should come from the
configured authentication system and should be

12
00:01:01.988 --> 00:01:09.171
available to the endpoint through the
normal ASP.NET Core request context.

13
00:01:09.251 --> 00:01:15.873
Authentication middleware behavior and
order should be understood.

14
00:01:15.953 --> 00:01:24.035
The application needs the authentication
middleware to establish the caller before

15
00:01:24.115 --> 00:01:28.941
authorization decisions depend on that
identity.

16
00:01:29.021 --> 00:01:35.418
Route groups and endpoint metadata can
make requirements visible,

17
00:01:35.498 --> 00:01:43.243
but the pipeline still needs to be
configured correctly so those requirements are

18
00:01:43.323 --> 00:01:45.343
enforced at runtime.

19
00:01:45.423 --> 00:01:53.392
Cookie-authenticated browser APIs and
bearer-token APIs have different security

20
00:01:53.472 --> 00:01:55.155
considerations.

21
00:01:55.235 --> 00:02:02.643
Cookie-based APIs may involve browser
session behavior, SameSite settings,

22
00:02:02.723 --> 00:02:09.682
CSRF prevention for unsafe requests, and
frontend integration choices.

23
00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:16.945
Bearer-token APIs usually expect the
client to present a token explicitly,

24
00:02:17.025 --> 00:02:25.218
which changes the browser credential model
but still requires careful token handling

25
00:02:25.298 --> 00:02:27.206
and authorization.

26
00:02:27.286 --> 00:02:34.245
Authentication failure behavior should be
predictable for API clients.

27
00:02:34.325 --> 00:02:39.039
A browser page may expect a redirect in
some flows,

28
00:02:39.119 --> 00:02:46.415
while an API client may expect a status
response with a clear but minimal error

29
00:02:46.495 --> 00:02:47.168
shape.

30
00:02:47.248 --> 00:02:53.197
The response should not disclose sensitive
internal details,

31
00:02:53.277 --> 00:03:00.460
and it should be consistent enough for
clients and operators to understand.

32
00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:06.376
A good Minimal API baseline documents
which schemes are used,

33
00:03:06.456 --> 00:03:12.741
which endpoints require identity, how
failures are represented,

34
00:03:12.821 --> 00:03:18.994
and how development behavior differs from
production behavior.

35
00:03:19.074 --> 00:03:27.380
That clarity prevents identity assumptions
from becoming hidden application policy.
